<section class="tale-hero" id="top"><div class="tale-hero-copy"><nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/index.html">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/stories.html">Stories</a><span>/</span><span>Queen Madalasa</span></nav><p class="eyebrow">MARKANDEYA PURANA / THE EDUCATION OF THE SONS</p><h1>The wisdom of the Self.<br><em>The duty of a king.</em></h1><p class="tale-deck">A queen teaches her children that they are more than body and title. When her youngest must govern, the same wisdom becomes a lesson in justice, restraint and care.</p><div class="tale-meta"><span>Markandeya Purana · Cantos XXIV–XXIX</span><span>About {{ storyCatalog.madalasa.minutes }} min read</span><a href="/sources.html#madalasa">View sources</a></div><a class="button" href="#homecoming">Begin the story</a></div><figure class="tale-hero-art"><img src="/assets/images/madalasa-atman.png" alt="Cinematic artistic interpretation of Queen Madalasa teaching her infant son in a sunlit royal chamber" width="1200" height="900" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>A MOTHER'S FIRST TEACHING</figcaption></figure></section>
<div class="tale-introduction"><p>From the <strong>Markandeya Purana, Cantos XXIV–XXIX</strong>. The account follows the story of Queen Madalasa, her husband Ritadhvaja (also called Kuvalayashva), their three elder sons and their youngest, Alarka. This is an original English prose retelling of the owner-supplied F. E. Pargiter translation, not a verbatim reproduction.</p></div>
<section class="story-characters" aria-labelledby="characters-title"><p class="eyebrow">MEET THE CHARACTERS</p><h2 id="characters-title">Characters in this story</h2><dl><div><dt>Queen Madalasa</dt><dd>A mother and teacher whose understanding of the Self shapes the education of four sons.</dd></div><div><dt>Ritadhvaja / Kuvalayashva</dt><dd>Her husband and king; he asks that their youngest son be educated for the duties of rulership.</dd></div><div><dt>Vikranta</dt><dd>The first son, taught from infancy to distinguish the Self from changing identity.</dd></div><div><dt>Subahu and Shatrumardana</dt><dd>The second and third sons, also guided toward detachment by their mother.</dd></div><div><dt>Prince Alarka</dt><dd>The youngest son, whose education embraces self-control, leadership, duty and hospitality.</dd></div><div><dt>Ashvatara and Shatrujit</dt><dd>The Naga king who aided the couple's reunion, and Ritadhvaja’s father, whose death precedes the new reign.</dd></div></dl></section>
